            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "It was still quite unclear to me what is actually happening, and the Cython docs don't seem to cover this either (not surprising, since it's supposed to be internals-only). So I did a bit of digging and had a conversation with Claude (which struggled for a while too, but saying \"that doesn't make sense\" enough times gets it there). So here is the Q&A that would have helped me:\n\n\n## What is actually clashing? Where are the clashing objects defined, and how are they shared between extension modules?\n\nThe clash is between two incompatible C struct layouts for the same shared runtime type, `cython_function_or_method`. Every Cython-compiled module carries its own generated copy of the code defining this type, but at import time it doesn't necessarily use its own copy: it looks up a hidden registry module in `sys.modules` (named `_cython_<version+ABI-suffixes>`, process-global) and, if a type with that name is already published there, adopts it after verifying its `basicsize` matches what this module's code was compiled against. The first module to import publishes its version; everyone else reuses it.\n\nIn 3.3.0, the struct gained a field that exists only for `Py_LIMITED_API` targets < 3.11, but the registry name didn't encode that split — so a `<3.11`-target module and a `3.11`-target module compute the same registry key with different struct sizes (160 vs 152 bytes), and whichever imports second fails the size check with the `TypeError`. It's a runtime registry-key collision, not a linker/symbol issue.\n\n## Why does this sharing mechanism exist at all?\n\nSo that all Cython modules of the same Cython version in one process agree on a single canonical type object, instead of each minting a structurally-identical-but-distinct one. That preserves cross-package type identity: `isinstance` and `type(...) is type(...)` behave consistently, identity-based fast paths work across module boundaries (most notably `await`/`yield from` driving another module's Cython coroutine/generator directly through C-level machinery — the same registry also shares the generator/coroutine types), and a few identity-dependent behaviors (e.g. auto-wrapping a recognized Cython function in `staticmethod` when assigned as `__new__`) work uniformly.\n\nThe mechanism is old and universal — it long predates Limited API support and applies to regular builds too; it was always safe there because, within one process, non-abi3 modules necessarily target the same interpreter version, so \"same key\" implied \"same layout\" for free. The Limited API broke that invariant, since abi3 modules in one process can target different minimum versions.\n\n## What are the main impacts of the fix (#7916) for packages targeting the Stable ABI?\n\nThe fix keys the registry on the Limited API target version itself, so modules with different targets no longer share the type — the bug class disappears by construction (no more manual tracking of layout-affecting `#if`s), and mixed-target imports always succeed.\n\nThe cost is type fragmentation across target boundaries: Cython code no longer recognizes functions from a differently-targeted module as CyFunctions, so cyfunc-specific optimizations and fast paths are skipped there (falling back to generic protocols — correct, just slower), plus minor behavioral edges (cross-boundary `isinstance` failing, the `__new__`/`staticmethod` case). In practice the performance cost is modest for abi3 builds, since the biggest identity-gated call fast path (vectorcall) is compiled out in Limited API mode anyway, and it only applies when objects of these types actually flow between two Cython packages with different targets.",
